  data: {
    LLM: "Keep your Kings at the bottom of the Waste piles! Since every foundation ends with a King, a King is the 'Deadest' card in the game. If you place a King on top of a 5, that 5 is lost until the King is cleared (which can only happen at the very end).\n\nWaste Pile Organization: Dedicate each of the 4 Waste piles to specific card sequences or values. For example, use Pile 1 for high cards (J, Q, K), Pile 2 for mid-cards, and Piles 3 and 4 for sequences that match the Foundations' intervals. If you see a card that is needed soon in the 'Steps of 3' foundation, put it in a dedicated pile.\n\nLook-Ahead Calculation: Memorize the next 3-4 cards needed for EACH foundation. If you draw a 6, and Pile 3 (Step of 3) currently has a 3 on it, you can play the 6. But if Pile 3 has a 9, and the 6 is needed much later, decide if it's better to bury the 6 or keep it accessible.",
    Player: "Keep your Kings at the bottom of the Waste piles! Since every foundation ends with a King, a King is the 'Deadest' card in the game. If you place a King on top of a 5, that 5 is lost until the King is cleared (which can only happen at the very end).\n\nWaste Pile Organization: Dedicate each of the 4 Waste piles to specific card sequences or values. For example, use Pile 1 for high cards (J, Q, K), Pile 2 for mid-cards, and Piles 3 and 4 for sequences that match the Foundations' intervals. If you see a card that is needed soon in the 'Steps of 3' foundation, put it in a dedicated pile.\n\nLook-Ahead Calculation: Memorize the next 3-4 cards needed for EACH foundation. If you draw a 6, and Pile 3 (Step of 3) currently has a 3 on it, you can play the 6. But if Pile 3 has a 9, and the 6 is needed much later, decide if it's better to bury the 6 or keep it accessible.",
